# Harness dispatcher, reaper, and merge worker — one tick each, every minute.
# Install with:
#
#   sudo install -m 0644 /home/ubuntu/toliki/etc/dispatch.cron /etc/cron.d/harness-dispatch
#
# All three lines, or none. Dispatching without reaping stalls the queue: a
# finished run's tmux session keeps its name, and dispatch skips any issue that
# still has one, so issues that ended in a skip or a blocker are never
# re-offered. Reaping without dispatching frees slots nothing then claims. And
# dispatching without merging stalls the last mile: every clean epic parks at
# `ready-to-merge` with an open PR nothing lands, so the fleet keeps building
# against a main that never advances. The two blockers this file used to name —
# that unfreed slot and the stale claim ref a dead run leaves — are both answered by
# bin/reap.sh, which is why the reap line is here rather than a note saying it
# should be.
#
# Still NOT installed by bin/provision.sh: turning the box autonomous is a
# deliberate act, not a side effect of provisioning it.
#
# The interval is far shorter than an epic takes on purpose — an idle tick is
# one gh call per repo and exits in a second or two, so polling often just means
# a freed slot refills quickly. Measured on a 3-repo host: dispatch and merge
# ~2s, reap ~5s (it also does a git ls-remote per repo for claim refs). At one
# tick a minute that is a few percent of one core and well under 10% of both the
# 5000/hour REST budget and the 30/minute search cap.
#
# All three run at the same period deliberately. Dispatch cannot use a slot that
# reap has not freed yet, so a fast dispatch beside a slow reap just moves the
# queue's bottleneck rather than removing it — at MAX_PARALLEL_EPICS the box
# would still only turn over at the reaper's pace. Merge is the third link in
# the same chain: an issue blocked_by another stays unlaunchable until that
# blocker's PR lands and closes it, so merge latency is dependency latency.
#
# They used to be offset off each other's minutes so a sweep was not killing
# sessions in the instant a tick was counting them. At a one-minute period there
# is no offset left to give (cron's floor is the minute), and the collision the
# offset avoided is now cheaper than it was: the worst case is that dispatch
# counts a session reap is about to kill, reads the host as full, and skips one
# launch — recovered on the next tick a minute later, where it used to cost
# five. Reap only ever kills sessions whose issue already went terminal and sat
# untouched for TERMINAL_SETTLE_MINUTES, so there is no race against a live run.
# Real overlap is still handled by flock inside dispatch.sh and merge-worker.sh,
# not by the interval; note a merge that runs long (it waits on CI, ~30 min cap)
# makes every tick behind it log "still draining" until it finishes, which is
# reporting, not a fault. merge-tick.sh (not merge-worker.sh directly) is what
# keeps the repo list out of this file: it fans one worker out per repo in
# etc/repos.conf.
#
# cron hands a job PATH=/usr/bin:/bin and nothing else, which has neither
# claude (~/.local/bin, see provision.sh) nor node's global bins. This line has
# to carry BOTH: node is the pane's own process (the pipeline orchestrator) and
# claude is every agent it spawns, resolved through the same inherited PATH.
# Without it the session launches, the pane can't find its command, and it
# shows up as `dead (at bash prompt)` in `./remote-control.sh ls` — the same
# symptom as the workspace-trust trap, from a completely different cause.
PATH=/home/ubuntu/.local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
* * * * * ubuntu /home/ubuntu/toliki/bin/dispatch.sh >> /home/ubuntu/dispatch.log 2>&1
* * * * * ubuntu /home/ubuntu/toliki/bin/reap.sh >> /home/ubuntu/reap.log 2>&1
* * * * * ubuntu /home/ubuntu/toliki/bin/merge-tick.sh >> /home/ubuntu/merge.log 2>&1

# The fourth line is NOT part of the "all three or none" contract above, and the
# distinction is worth keeping straight: those three are one mechanism and each
# one missing breaks the other two. This one is an observer. It starts nothing,
# frees nothing and merges nothing, so a box running it alone is just an idle box
# keeping a log — it is host hygiene, the same class as the logrotate config and
# the docker GC policy, and it can be dropped or added without touching the
# pipeline's behavior.
#
# It earns its minute because MAX_PARALLEL_EPICS can only be raised honestly by
# watching a real drain, and the failures that decide the ceiling are conditional
# — the real-DB tier booting a Supabase stack under an already-loaded box, or
# several epics reaching their review fan-out together. Both are invisible to a
# snapshot taken by hand after the fact. Read it back with bin/resource-report.sh
# (default: last 24h); the sampler costs ~0.4s and appends one line per tick.
* * * * * ubuntu /home/ubuntu/toliki/bin/resource-log.sh >> /home/ubuntu/resource-log.err 2>&1

# Hygiene class too, like the sampler, and hourly rather than per-minute:
# bin/update-claude.sh moves the claude binary to the latest release when — and
# only when — nothing is running (launch.sh --check-idle, the cap's own count).
# Nothing else keeps that binary current: a headless `claude -p` never
# self-updates and provision.sh never upgrades a version already present, so
# without this line the CLI every phase spawns quietly ages until someone
# updates it by hand — and with it, the model aliases the pipelines' tier
# tables are written in. It holds dispatch's lock for the length of the
# install, so a tick landing in that window logs "previous tick still running"
# and moves on. A busy hour is simply skipped; the queue drains to empty often
# enough to catch up. Minute 37 is arbitrary — the lock, not the offset, keeps
# it clear of dispatch.
37 * * * * ubuntu /home/ubuntu/toliki/bin/update-claude.sh >> /home/ubuntu/update.log 2>&1
